{
  "slug": "median-of-two-sorted-arrays",
  "title": "Median of Two Arrays",
  "topic": "binary-search",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "pattern": "sort both arrays, then binary search on the partition index",
  "statement": "Given `nums1` and `nums2`, consider the multiset of all their values. Either array may hold its values in any order, so sort what you need as part of your solution. If the combined count is odd the median is the single middle value; if it is even the median is the average of the two middle values. Return the median as a floating-point number, a `double` in the compiled languages. A median that lands on a whole number is still a `double`, so `2` and `2.0` denote the same result and the judge compares the value rather than the way it is written.\n\nFor `nums1 = [3, 1]` and `nums2 = [2]` the answer is 2, from the sorted order 1, 2, 3. For `nums1 = [2, 1]` and `nums2 = [4, 3]` the answer is 2.5, the average of the two middle values 2 and 3.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums1.length <= 200`, `1 <= nums2.length <= 200`, `-10000 <= nums1[i] <= 10000`, `-10000 <= nums2[i] <= 10000`, and the values of each array are given in arbitrary order.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums1 = [-10000], nums2 = [-10000]",
      "output": "-10000",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums1 = [929], nums2 = [-1417, -4706, 4478, 8077, 4014]",
      "output": "2471.5",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums1: number[], nums2: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Concatenate the two arrays, sort the result and read the middle position, or the average of the two middle positions. That is O((m + n) log(m + n)) and is already a complete, correct answer; start there.",
    "To avoid building a third array, sort each input on its own first. The answer then depends only on where the union is cut in half: if the shorter sorted array contributes its first `i` elements to the left half, the longer one must contribute exactly `ceil((m + n)/2) - i`.",
    "Binary search that `i` over `[0, m]` on the shorter sorted array. The cut is right when `a[i-1] <= b[j]` and `b[j-1] <= a[i]`; if `a[i-1] > b[j]` move left, otherwise move right, and treat the sides outside an array as minus and plus infinity. Return a whole result as an integer so the answer matches the judge's expected text."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The direct version concatenates the two arrays, sorts the result and reads the middle position, averaging the two middle values when the combined length is even; that is O((m + n) log(m + n)) time and O(m + n) extra memory. The optimal never builds the concatenation. It sorts each array on its own, then swaps them so the shorter one is `a`; if the left half of the merged order holds `ceil((m + n) / 2)` items, a split of `a` after `i` items forces the split of `b` after `j = ceil((m + n) / 2) - i`. That split is correct when nothing on the left exceeds anything on the right, that is `a[i-1] <= b[j]` and `b[j-1] <= a[i]`. Binary searching `i` over `[0, m]` finds it, and the answer is then `max(a[i-1], b[j-1])` for an odd total or the average of that maximum and `min(a[i], b[j])` for an even total, with the sides outside an array treated as minus and plus infinity.",
    "why_optimal": "Sorting the two arrays costs O(m log m + n log n) and is unavoidable, because either input may arrive in any order and the middle of a multiset cannot be located without order. What the partition search still removes is the merged array: it runs over the shorter array, so it costs O(log(min(m, n))) comparisons and stores four boundary values instead of a third array. The number of elements taken from `a` is the only degree of freedom the answer depends on: for each `i` the split of `b` is forced, and the validity test is monotone in `i` (a too small left side pushes `a[i-1] > b[j]`, a too large one pushes `b[j-1] > a[i]`), so halving is the optimal way to locate it.",
    "pitfalls": "The judge compares the answer as JSON text, so the two languages have to agree on the representation and not merely on the value: JavaScript has a single number type and prints a whole median as `2`, while Python prints the float `2.0` as `2.0`. A Python solution that computes `(a + b) / 2` and returns the result unchanged is therefore marked wrong whenever the median is whole, which is why both Python implementations convert a whole result to `int` and leave a half-integer as a float. Swapping to put the shorter array first is mandatory: otherwise `j = (m + n + 1) / 2 - i` can exceed the longer array's length and `b[j]` reads out of bounds. The out-of-range sides must be minus and plus infinity, not 0, or the comparisons fail as soon as one array holds the smallest values. The loop must run while `lo <= hi`, otherwise the final partition is never tested, and the updates must be `hi = i - 1` when `a[i-1] > b[j]` and `lo = i + 1` otherwise. Both arrays must be sorted before the partition search; if one is left in its argument order the search answers a different question than the concatenate-and-sort version and the two implementations disagree. Finally, an even combined length must return the average, a half-integer, not an index into the merged order."
  },
